docs(adr): decide how the hot tier is pruned, and why not yet (#118)

#118 decided the hot tier is prunable and that a sealed range becomes one marker
row. What it did not settle is how a row leaves innsegl.events, and measured
against the running deployment the answer is that it cannot: DELETE, a ranged
DELETE, TRUNCATE, UPDATE, and DELETE from chain are each refused IN001 as the
SCHEMA OWNER, which is the most privileged role this deployment has.

So "a thousand rows become one" and "no row is ever deleted" cannot both be true
of one table, and that fork is what the ADR exists to settle. Partitioning and
detaching would work — DDL is not caught by a statement trigger — and it buys
the compression by giving a scheduled job a credential that can drop the events
table. Every other decision here has kept those apart.

The arithmetic settles it rather than the principle. 5,212 events, 9.3 MB
including indexes, 4,493 events a day, about 2.8 GB projected over twelve
months. Pruning would reclaim under three gigabytes a year.

Two measurements make that stronger, not weaker: the rate is over 1.2 days of
unusually heavy development and is therefore an UPPER bound, and the bodies are
within budget — tool_call averages 693 bytes canonical against IP §6's 1 KB
target and is 89% of all events. At 1,819 bytes per event of total relation
size, roughly 2.6x the body, the INDEXES are most of the footprint rather than
the events; that is the cheaper place to look if the hot tier ever must shrink.

THE SHAPE IS DECIDED AND THE WORK IS NOT DONE, deliberately. A marker earns its
place only when the rows it stands in for are gone; with them present it is a
code path no query takes, on a table nothing writes, maintained until needed. So
the ADR records how pruning happens when it happens, names three measurements
that would reopen it, and #118 stays open against those rather than being closed
as done or built as something inert.

The threat-model half — that this enforcement binds DML and not DDL, which roles
can reach DDL, and why that is acceptable — is in doc 04, which does not ship.
docs/adr/ does.

